<section style="padding-top:40px">
  <div class="wrap" style="max-width:820px">

    <div class="prose">
      <p class="lead"><strong>Three specs decide it: weight, shaft flex and balance.</strong> For a club player that means 4U or 5U, a medium or flexible shaft, and even balance, and you can buy all of it for £40 to £70. Above about £80 the market starts selling you a professional's racket instead: heavier in the head, stiffer in the shaft, and increasingly with no strings in the box.</p>

      <p>Get it wrong and you will spend £150 finding out that the racket was never the thing holding you back.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:30px">
      <h4>The short version</h4>
      <ul style="margin:10px 0 0">
        <li><strong>Buy 4U or 5U, medium or flexible shaft, even balance.</strong> That is the whole club specification and it is available from about £40.</li>
        <li><strong>£40 to £70 is the band.</strong> Above roughly £80 the specs do not stop improving, they start pointing at somebody else.</li>
        <li><strong>Check whether it comes strung.</strong> Above £120 most rackets do not, and a restring is another £12 to £25.</li>
        <li><strong>Head-heavy will not make your smash faster.</strong> That is measured, not opinion.</li>
        <li><strong>The racket is not your limiting factor.</strong> Where you hit the shuttle and how fast you swing account for most of it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <p style="margin-top:22px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.95rem">Written for adults buying a first or second badminton racket in the UK, playing club or social badminton. Prices and stock figures are from a survey of two UK retailer catalogues taken on 23 August 2026; the method is at the foot of this guide.</p>

    <div class="guide-toc" style="margin-top:26px">
      <h4>In this guide</h4>
      <a href="#which-specs">Which specs actually decide it?</a>
      <a href="#laws">What do the Laws require?</a>
      <a href="#weight">Weight: what do 3U, 4U and 5U mean?</a>
      <a href="#balance">Does a head-heavy racket make you hit harder?</a>
      <a href="#flex">Shaft flex: stiff or flexible?</a>
      <a href="#grip">Grip size: does it matter?</a>
      <a href="#strung">Does it come strung?</a>
      <a href="#spend">How much should you spend?</a>
      <a href="#cheap">What does the cheap shelf actually sell you?</a>
      <a href="#ignore">What should you ignore?</a>
      <a href="#demo">Can you try before you buy?</a>
      <a href="#does-it-matter">Does the racket matter as much as you think?</a>
      <a href="#faq">Frequently asked questions</a>
    </div>

    <div class="prose" style="margin-top:34px">

      <h2 id="which-specs">Which specs actually decide it?</h2>
      <p>Three specs decide it: <strong>weight, shaft flex and balance</strong>. Manufacturers publish five fields, adding grip size and a maximum string tension, and every retailer guide dutifully walks you through all five as though they were five decisions. They are not.</p>
      <p>Grip size is a fit check rather than a choice, and the UK market largely makes it for you. String tension is a real decision but not one you make when you buy, because it recurs every few months and because under about £120 the racket arrives already strung. That leaves three.</p>
      <p>There is a sixth thing that matters more than two of the five and appears on nobody's spec sheet: <strong>whether the racket comes with strings in it at all.</strong></p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/racket-anatomy.svg" alt="A badminton racket seen from the side with its parts labelled: the stringed head, the throat, the shaft, and the grip. The balance point is marked as a distance in millimetres measured from the butt of the handle, with an even-balance racket sitting near the middle of the shaft, head-light shorter and head-heavy longer." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">Balance is a measured distance from the butt of the handle, not a style. Everything a retailer lists describes this frame; only three of the numbers change your decision.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="laws">What do the Laws require?</h2>
      <p>The Laws of Badminton regulate the shape of a racket and nothing else. Law 4 sets the frame at no more than 680 mm long and 230 mm wide, the stringed area at no more than 280 mm by 220 mm, and requires the racket to be <em>"free of attached objects and protrusions"</em> beyond those limiting wear, vibration or weight distribution.</p>
      <p>Nothing in the Laws mentions weight, balance, shaft flex, grip size or string tension. Every number printed on the shaft is a manufacturer's own convention, and that matters because you will read that the U-rating is a BWF classification. It is not, and Law 4 is the proof: there is no governing-body standard for how heavy a racket may be.</p>

      <h2 id="weight">Weight: what do 3U, 4U and 5U mean?</h2>
      <p>The U and W numbers are unstrung weight bands, and they run backwards: <strong>3U is 85 to 89 g, 4U is 80 to 84 g, 5U is 75 to 79 g.</strong> Lower number, heavier racket. Buy 4U, or 5U if you are unsure or coming back from a wrist or shoulder problem.</p>
      <p><strong>The trap is that the scales run in opposite directions between brands.</strong> Yonex and Victor use U, counting down as the racket gets lighter. Li-Ning uses W, counting up. A 5U and a W1 are the same weight. A 3U and a W3 are the same weight. Two rackets labelled 3 are ten grams apart.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" style="overflow-x:auto">
      <table class="cmp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Unstrung weight</th><th>Yonex / Victor</th><th>Li-Ning</th><th>Who it suits</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>75-79 g</td><td>5U</td><td>W1</td><td>Easiest to swing. Fine for anyone, and the safer choice if you are unsure.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>80-84 g</td><td>4U</td><td>W2</td><td>The club default, and what most rackets from £30 up now are.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>85-89 g</td><td>3U</td><td>W3</td><td>Heavier. Wants a developed swing to be worth carrying.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <p>Victor adds one more wrinkle: it labels grips with S-numbers rather than G-numbers, so a Victor S3 is a G5. Nothing about this is standardised, which is itself worth knowing before you compare two listings.</p>

      <h2 id="balance">Does a head-heavy racket make you hit harder?</h2>
      <p>A head-heavy racket does not make the shuttle leave faster, and that is measured rather than argued. A 2023 study in <em>Scientific Reports</em> had twenty experienced players hit 1,500 smashes with five rackets of <strong>identical mass</strong> whose weight distribution was varied across a range wider than the commercial one. As the weight moved towards the head, racket head speed fell significantly. Shuttle speed did not change at all.</p>
      <p>So balance changes how a racket feels to swing, not how fast the shuttle leaves it. Head-heavy feels solid and planted and is more tiring over three hours. Head-light feels quick in the hand and less stable through a clear. Even balance is the sensible default, and it is what Victor's own beginner guidance recommends for a player without a settled style.</p>
      <p>Two honest limits on that study. It held mass constant, so it tells you about <em>where</em> the weight sits and not about how much there is. And its players were regional to international, which is true of every piece of badminton research in this guide.</p>

      <h2 id="flex">Shaft flex: stiff or flexible?</h2>
      <p>Buy a medium or flexible shaft, and know that the manufacturers openly disagree about which. Yonex's entry tier is built around a flexible shaft; Victor's own beginner guide recommends 4 to 7 on its one-to-ten handling scale, which is moderate stiffness rather than flex. There is no cross-brand standard here at all, and Victor does not even use the words stiff and flexible.</p>
      <p>The reason it matters is that a shaft bends and then straightens during the swing, and that adds racket head speed. One study of national and international players found shaft deflection increased head velocity by 13.2% during a full smash, with the effect depending on the relationship between the player, the racket's mass distribution and the shaft. A stiff shaft only repays a swing fast enough and well-timed enough to load it. If yours is not yet, a stiff shaft is a frame that does less of the work.</p>

      <h2 id="grip">Grip size: does it matter?</h2>
      <p>Grip size matters less than the amount written about it suggests, and the market has largely decided for you. Of the 234 adult rackets in our survey, 111 were G5, 44 were G4 and 36 were G6, so most listings offer one size and that size is G5. An overgrip adds roughly half a millimetre, which is enough to move you most of the way towards the next size up anyway.</p>
      <p>The evidence is split, too. A 2006 study found that altering grip size within a quarter of an inch of the recommended fit did not significantly change forearm muscle activity and may not be a meaningful risk factor for tennis elbow. A 2014 simulation study found an optimal grip diameter that reduces the grip force required. Nobody should tell you with confidence that your grip size caused your elbow pain.</p>

      <h2 id="strung">Does it come strung?</h2>
      <p>Check whether the racket comes with strings before you compare it on price, because above about £120 most rackets do not come with strings and no listing puts that on the spec table. In our survey the share sold frame-only rose steadily with price: none below £60, 19% at £80 to £99, 60% at £120 to £139, and every single racket above £180.</p>
      <p>A UK restring costs roughly £12 to £25. So a £180 frame costs £192 to £205 before you have played with it, and you are choosing a tension on no evidence, because you have not yet hit anything with the racket.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Worked example: what a £180 racket actually costs</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">£180 for the frame. It arrives unstrung, because at that price they all do. A restring is £12 to £25 depending on the string, so call it £195. You pick a tension you have no basis for picking, because you have never hit a shuttle with this frame.</p>
        <p style="margin-top:10px">The £50 racket next to it arrives strung, playable that evening, and its published specs are the ones that suit you. The difference, £145, is about six restrings, or a pair of court shoes and change.</p>
      </div>

      <h2 id="spend">How much should you spend?</h2>
      <p><strong>Spend between £40 and £70 on the racket.</strong> By £40 you can buy 4U, even balance, a medium shaft, graphite and strings in the box, which is the complete club specification. Between there and £250 nothing on that list improves.</p>
      <p>We surveyed 234 adult rackets across two UK specialist retailers to check this, because no published guide answers it and every one that tries is written by somebody with £200 rackets in stock. What the catalogue shows is not a plateau. It is a market that steadily stops selling you the thing you want.</p>
      <div class="tablewrap" style="overflow-x:auto">
      <table class="cmp-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Price band</th><th>Rackets</th><th>4U or 5U</th><th>Head-heavy</th><th>Stiff shaft</th><th>Marketed to beginners</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Under £30</td><td>29</td><td>3%</td><td>17%</td><td>7%</td><td>93%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£30-49</td><td>35</td><td>68%</td><td>29%</td><td>6%</td><td>54%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£50-79</td><td>32</td><td>75%</td><td>34%</td><td>9%</td><td>31%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£80-119</td><td>57</td><td>65%</td><td>54%</td><td>18%</td><td>0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£120-179</td><td>58</td><td>66%</td><td>55%</td><td>40%</td><td>0%</td></tr>
          <tr><td>£180+</td><td>23</td><td>43%</td><td>61%</td><td>69%</td><td>4%</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      </div>
      <p>Count the rackets that match the whole club specification at once, and the picture is starker. Sixteen of the 234 are 4U or 5U with even balance, a medium or flexible shaft, and strings included. <strong>Thirteen of those sixteen cost under £80. None of the twenty-three rackets above £180 match.</strong></p>
      <p>There is no conspiracy in this. Premium rackets are designed for and marketed through professionals, and a professional's racket is stiffer, heavier in the head, and sold unstrung so the player can choose their own tension. The £200 racket is better at being a professional's racket. The mistake is assuming that axis points at you.</p>

      <figure style="margin:26px 0 0">
        <img src="/img/diagrams/racket-price-curve.svg" alt="Two charts against price band. The first shows how many rackets match the full club specification of 4U or 5U weight, even balance, medium or flexible shaft and strings included: it peaks at seven rackets in the fifty to seventy-nine pound band and falls to zero above a hundred and eighty pounds. The second shows the share of rackets sold as a frame only with no strings, rising steadily from zero per cent below sixty pounds to one hundred per cent above a hundred and eighty pounds." style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:12px" />
        <figcaption style="margin-top:10px;color:var(--muted-2);font-size:.92rem">234 adult rackets, two UK retailers, 23 August 2026. The club specification is concentrated under £80, and the strings come out of the box as the price climbs.</figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2 id="cheap">What does the cheap shelf actually sell you?</h2>
      <p>The cheap shelf sells you a heavier racket, which is the opposite of what most people assume. Every racket under £24 in the survey was 1U or 2U, meaning 90 g and up, including the budget lines from brands you have heard of. The first 4U appears at £24.95, and from about £31 upwards 4U is simply standard.</p>
      <p>So the concrete upgrade from a £15 supermarket racket is not carbon fibre or a bigger sweet spot. It is dropping ten to fifteen grams, which is the difference between a frame you have to haul into position and one you can put where you want it.</p>

      <h2 id="ignore">What should you ignore?</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Isometric heads.</strong> The squarer head shape is a real design and the manufacturer's own claim is a 7% larger sweet spot against a conventional round frame. It is also thirty years old and now effectively universal, so it does not tell you anything about the rackets you are choosing between.</li>
        <li><strong>Signature editions.</strong> A player's name and a different colourway. One in our survey listed at £247.</li>
        <li><strong>"Match the racket to your playing style."</strong> Reasonable advice for somebody who has one. If you are buying a first or second racket you do not yet, and the manufacturers' own answer for that case is even balance.</li>
        <li><strong>Material names.</strong> Everything above about £30 is graphite with something added, and the something is not comparable between brands.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>One number on the spec sheet is worth respecting: the <strong>maximum recommended string tension</strong>. Most rackets in the survey were rated to 28 lb. That one has consequences if you exceed it, because it is the frame's limit rather than a marketing figure.</p>

      <h2 id="demo">Can you try before you buy?</h2>
      <p>Several UK retailers run demo schemes, and the terms differ enough to matter. One specialist takes a full deposit for up to fourteen days and refunds it minus £20 per racket, allows two rackets at a time, requires the grip sleeve to stay on, and currently excludes two of the largest brands. Others charge a flat fee of £8 to £15, sometimes refundable against a purchase, sometimes not.</p>
      <p>It is worth doing if you are choosing between two rackets you have narrowed down. It is not worth doing to discover whether you like badminton rackets. And your club is the cheaper option: most club nights contain several people who will happily let you hit six shots with theirs.</p>

      <h2 id="does-it-matter">Does the racket matter as much as you think?</h2>
      <p>The racket matters much less than the amount of advice about it implies. Across 2,386 smashes by international players, where the shuttle struck the strings alone accounted for about a quarter of the variation in shuttle speed, and impact location together with racket head speed accounted for 89% of it. Where you hit it and how fast you swing is the game. The spec sheet is a rounding error on that.</p>
      <p>It is also worth saying plainly that <strong>nobody has measured any of this at club level.</strong> Every study in this guide used regional to international players. They are the best evidence available and they all point the same way, but a recreational player is not who was tested.</p>
      <p>If you want somewhere better to put the money, the honest answer is a mixed one. Badminton's injuries are overwhelmingly in the lower limb, with one review of 28 studies putting it at 52% and ankle injuries the most common single category. But the research on badminton shoes reports biomechanical measures rather than injury rates, so <strong>nobody has shown that buying better shoes prevents those injuries.</strong> What is established is where you get hurt, not what fixes it. Buy shoes you can move in, and treat that as a comfort decision with a plausible mechanism rather than a proven one.</p>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:26px">
        <h4>What to check on the listing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Four things, in this order. Everything else on the page is describing the racket rather than helping you choose it.</p>
        <ol style="margin:12px 0 0">
          <li>Weight: 4U or 5U (Li-Ning: W2 or W1).</li>
          <li>Shaft: medium or flexible.</li>
          <li>Balance: even.</li>
          <li>Strings: included, not frame-only.</li>
        </ol>
        <p style="margin-top:12px">If all four are true and it is between £40 and £70, buy it and go and play.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>If you only do one thing</h4>
        <p style="margin-top:8px">Check whether the price includes strings. It is the one number that changes what you actually pay, and it is on no spec table.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="guide-callout" style="margin-top:24px">
        <h4>Fair games, without the admin</h4>
        <p>Whatever you are holding, the evening works better when the games are even. ePegboard picks balanced pairings on a club night and keeps the mixing varied, so people play with a range of partners rather than the same three. Free for clubs.</p>
